\subsection{Scanning a Schedule with Holes in Its Image}

An invertible affine schedule over the rationals need not map integer iteration points onto all integer points in the scheduled space. Even exact rational bounds for the image therefore do not suffice to generate code. For a triangular schedule, a divisibility constraint becomes a stride in the generated inner loop.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} The original domain is the integer rectangle $0\le i<N$, $0\le j<M$, with nonnegative runtime bounds. A dependence test has already established legality of the lexicographic schedule $(s,t)=(i,i+kj)$ for a positive integer constant $k$. Assume exact arithmetic for deriving bounds and an index representation wide enough for execution. The generated loop must execute each original instance once and in scheduled order.

\begin{sudocode}[Scan with the required residue]
\Require $N,M\geq 0$, $k>0$, statement $S$
\Ensure Each scheduled integer instance executes once
\If{$N=0$ or $M=0$}
  \State \Return \Comment{The domain is empty}
\EndIf
\For{$s=0,\ldots,N-1$}
  \Let{t}{s}
  \Let{U}{s+k(M-1)}
  \While{$t\leq U$}
    \Let{j}{(t-s)/k}
    \State Execute $S(s,j)$
    \If{$t=U$}
      \State \textbf{break}
    \EndIf
    \State $t\gets t+k$ \Comment{Keep the residue of $s$}
  \EndWhile
\EndFor
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} For $N=2$, $M=3$, and $k=2$, the generated schedule visits $(0,0),(0,2),(0,4),(1,1),(1,3),(1,5)$ and executes six original points. The point $(0,1)$ lies inside the rational image but would require $j=1/2$ and is never visited. The offset of the residue changes with the outer index: the second row uses odd rather than even $t$.

\textbf{Invariant.} At each inner-loop entry, $t=s+kj$ for the next valid $j$. The stride gives one step per actual instance, compared to testing all $k(M-1)+1$ candidates in a dense scan. The final-point test also avoids needing a representable value beyond $U$. For a general integer schedule, residues may depend on several outer coordinates or come from multiple disjoint pieces; this single-stride construction applies only to the stated triangular form.

\section{Parametric Polyhedra}\label{sec:parametric-polyhedra}

In practice, loop bounds often depend on runtime parameters (e.g., array sizes). \emph{Parametric polyhedra} handle this by treating parameters as symbolic constants.

\begin{definition}[Parametric Polyhedron]\label{def:parametric-polyhedron}
A \textbf{parametric polyhedron} is a set of integer points defined by constraints involving both iteration variables and parameters:
\[
\mathcal{P}(\vec{p}) = \{\vec{x} \in \mathbb{Z}^n \mid A\vec{x} + B\vec{p} + \vec{c} \geq 0\}
\]
where $\vec{p}$ is the vector of parameters.
\end{definition}

Parametric polyhedra enable the polyhedral model to handle loops with runtime-determined bounds, which is essential for practical applications.

\section{Legality of Transformations}\label{sec:transformation-legality}

\begin{definition}[Legality Condition]\label{def:legality}
An affine transformation $T$ is \textbf{legal} for a SCoP if it preserves all dependences. Formally, for every dependence $(\vec{i}, \vec{j})$ where $\vec{i}$ executes before $\vec{j}$:
\[
T(\vec{j}) >_{\mathit{lex}} T(\vec{i})
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Farkas Lemma for Legality]\label{thm:farkas}
By Farkas' lemma, the legality condition can be checked by solving a linear program: for each dependence polyhedron, verify that the schedule satisfies the dependence constraints.
\end{theorem}

\section{Integration with Compilers}\label{sec:polyhedral-compilers}

\subsection{Polly}\label{sec:polly}

\emph{Polly} is the polyhedral optimizer for LLVM. It:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Detects SCoPs in the LLVM IR.
  \item Extracts the polyhedral representation (iteration domains, access relations).
  \item Applies optimizations (tiling, fusion, interchange) using the Pluto algorithm.
  \item Generates optimized code using the ISL code generator.
  \item Integrates the optimized code back into the LLVM IR.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Graphite}\label{sec:graphite}

\emph{Graphite} is GCC's polyhedral optimization framework. It operates on GCC's GIMPLE IR and uses the CLooG code generator.

\begin{historical}
The polyhedral model was pioneered by Quiller\'e and Rajopadhye~\cite{quillere1991optimal} (1991) and Feautrier~\cite{feautrier1992dataflow} (1992). The Pluto algorithm of Bondhugula et al.~\cite{bondhugula2009pluto} (2009) brought automatic polyhedral optimization to practical compilers. Tools like CLooG (Cedercracken, 2007), ISL (Verdonck et al.), and Polly (Grosser et al.) have integrated polyhedral optimization into LLVM and GCC. The polyhedral model is now a standard component of high-performance compilers for scientific computing.
\end{historical}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:polyhedral-summary}

The polyhedral model provides a powerful mathematical framework for loop optimization. By representing iteration domains, access relations, and schedules as polyhedra and affine maps, it enables systematic exploration of loop transformations. The Pluto algorithm automatically finds optimal schedules that maximize parallelism and data locality. Tiling, skewing, and reversal are expressed as geometric operations on the iteration space. Parametric polyhedra handle runtime-determined loop bounds. Transformation legality is checked using Farkas' lemma. Code generation from polyhedra produces efficient loop nests that realize the optimized schedule. Modern compiler integrations (Polly for LLVM, Graphite for GCC) bring polyhedral optimization to production compilers. The polyhedral model is the state of the art for automatic loop optimization in scientific computing.

\section{The Pluto Algorithm in Detail}\label{sec:pluto-detail}

The \emph{Pluto} algorithm of Bondhugula et al.~\cite{bondhugula2009pluto} finds affine schedules that maximize parallelism and data locality for static control polyhedra.

\begin{definition}[Pluto Scheduling Problem]\label{def:pluto-problem}
Given a set of statements with iteration domains $\mathcal{D}_i$ and dependence relations $\mathcal{R}_k$, find affine schedule functions $\theta_i(\vec{x}) = C_i \cdot \vec{x} + \vec{c}_i$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Legality}: For each dependence $(\vec{x}, \vec{y}) \in \mathcal{R}_k$: $\theta_{target}(\vec{y}) \geq \theta_{source}(\vec{x})$ (lexicographically)
  \item \textbf{Optimality}: The schedule maximizes parallelism and locality
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Farkas Lemma for Dependence}\label{sec:farkas-detail}

\begin{definition}[Farkas Lemma]\label{def:farkas-lemma}
\textbf{Farkas' lemma} states that exactly one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item There exists $\vec{x}$ such that $A\vec{x} \leq \vec{b}$
  \item There exists $\vec{y} \geq 0$ such that $A^T\vec{y} = 0$ and $\vec{b}^T\vec{y} < 0$
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The Pluto algorithm uses Farkas' lemma to convert dependence constraints into linear constraints on the schedule coefficients:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Pluto Algorithm (Simplified)}
\label{alg:pluto}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Statements $S_1, \ldots, S_n$ with iteration domains $\mathcal{D}_i$ and dependences $\mathcal{R}_k$
\ensure Affine schedules $\theta_i$ satisfying legality and optimizing locality
\For{dimension $d = 1, 2, \ldots$}
  \State Set up ILP: find coefficients $C_i^{(d)}, c_i^{(d)}$ such that:
  \State \quad For each dependence $(\vec{x}, \vec{y}) \in \mathcal{R}_k$:
  \State \quad \quad $\theta_{target}^{(d)}(\vec{y}) - \theta_{source}^{(d)}(\vec{x}) \geq 0$
  \State \quad \quad (enforced via Farkas' lemma as linear constraints)
  \State Objective: maximize number of satisfied dependences at level $d$ (proximity heuristic)
  \State Solve ILP for $\theta^{(d)}$
  \If{no solution}
    \State Relax: allow $\theta^{(d)}$ to be non-carried for some dependences
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Tileability Detection}\label{sec:tileability}

\begin{definition}[Completely Permutable Loops]\label{def:permutable}
A loop nest is \textbf{completely permutable} if all dependence distance vectors are lexicographically non-negative. This means any permutation of the loops is legal, and the loop nest can be tiled.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pluto Tiling]\label{thm:pluto-tiling}
The Pluto algorithm detects tileable loop nests by finding schedule dimensions where all dependence distances are non-negative. When found, these dimensions can be tiled to improve data locality.
\end{theorem}

\section{Code Generation from Polyhedra}\label{sec:polyhedral-codegen}

Generating executable code from a polyhedral representation requires converting the abstract schedule back into concrete loop nests.

\begin{definition}[Polyhedral Code Generation]\label{def:poly-codegen}
\textbf{Polyhedral code generation} produces a loop nest that iterates over all integer points in the scheduled iteration domain, in the order specified by the schedule. The generated code must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Compute correct loop bounds for each dimension.
  \item Generate the statement bodies at the correct scheduled positions.
  \item Handle parametric bounds (runtime-determined loop limits).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Omega Library Approach}\label{sec:omega-approach}

\begin{definition}[Omega Test]\label{def:omega-test}
The \textbf{Omega test} is an algorithm for integer linear programming that decides whether a system of linear inequalities has an integer solution. It extends Fourier-Motzkin elimination with handling of integer divisibility constraints.
\end{definition}

\subsection{CLooG Code Generation}\label{sec:cloog-codegen}

\emph{CLooG} (Chunky Loop Generator) generates loop nests from polyhedral descriptions:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{CLooG Code Generation (Simplified)}
\label{alg:cloog}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Scheduled polyhedra $\{(\mathcal{D}_i, \theta_i)\}$ for each statement
\Ensure Loop nest implementing the schedule
\State Compute the \emph{scattering dimension} for each loop level
\For{each loop level $l$}
  \State Project the polyhedra onto dimension $l$
  \State Compute loop bounds using Fourier-Motzkin / Omega
  \State Generate loop header with computed bounds
\EndFor
\For{each leaf in the scan tree}
  \State Generate statement body
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Polyhedral Code Generation]\label{ex:poly-codegen}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Original:
  for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
      S: C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
  
  // After tiling (tile size T):
  for (t_i = 0; t_i < N; t_i += T)
    for (t_j = 0; t_j < N; t_j += T)
      for (i = t_i; i < min(t_i + T, N); i++)
        for (j = t_j; j < min(t_j + T, N); j++)
          S: C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
  
  // The polyhedral code generator computes:
  // - Outer loop bounds: 0 ≤ t_i ≤ N-1, step T
  // - Inner loop bounds: t_i ≤ i ≤ min(t_i + T - 1, N - 1)
  // - Guard expressions for boundary tiles
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Parametric Polyhedra}\label{sec:parametric-polyhedra}

\begin{definition}[Parametric Polyhedron]\label{def:parametric-polyhedron}
A \textbf{parametric polyhedron} is a polyhedron whose constraints involve parameters (symbols) in addition to variables:
\[
\mathcal{P}(\vec{p}) = \{(\vec{x}, \vec{p}) \mid A\vec{x} + B\vec{p} + \vec{c} \geq 0\}
\]
Parameters represent runtime values such as loop bounds, array sizes, or function arguments.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Parametric Iteration Domain]\label{ex:parametric-domain}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Loop: for (i = 0; i < N; i++) for (j = i; j < M; j++)
  // Iteration domain (parametric in N, M):
  // { [i, j] : 0 ≤ i ≤ N-1 ∧ i ≤ j ≤ M-1 }
  // This is a parametric polyhedron with parameters N and M
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Quasi-Polynomial Code Generation]\label{def:quasi-poly}
When parametric loop bounds involve integer division, the code generator produces \emph{quasi-polynomial} expressions:
\[
\text{bound}(p) = \lfloor \frac{a \cdot p + b}{c} \rfloor
\]
CLooG and ISL handle these by generating guarded code with different expressions for different parameter ranges.
\end{definition}

\section{Dependence Analysis in the Polyhedral Model}\label{sec:polyhedral-dependence}

\begin{definition}[Polyhedral Dependence]\label{def:poly-dependence}
A \textbf{polyhedral dependence} between two array accesses $A[f_S(\vec{x})]$ and $A[f_T(\vec{y})]$ exists if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\vec{x} \in \mathcal{D}_S$ and $\vec{y} \in \mathcal{D}_T$ (both iterations are valid)
  \item $f_S(\vec{x}) = f_T(\vec{y})$ (both access the same memory location)
  \item $\theta_S(\vec{x}) \leq \theta_T(\vec{y})$ (source executes before target in schedule)
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Polyhedral Dependence Analysis}
\label{alg:poly-dependence}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Source access $A[f_S(\vec{x})]$ at statement $S$, target access $A[f_T(\vec{y})]$ at statement $T$
\ensure Dependence polyhedron $\mathcal{D}_{S \to T}$
\State $\mathcal{D}_{S \to T} \gets \mathcal{D}_S \times \mathcal{D}_T$
\State Add constraint: $f_S(\vec{x}) = f_T(\vec{y})$ (same memory location)
\State Add constraint: $\theta_S(\vec{x}) \leq \theta_T(\vec{y})$ (schedule order)
\State Simplify using integer linear programming (Omega/ISL)
\If{$\mathcal{D}_{S \to T} = \emptyset$}
  \State \Return no dependence
\Else
  \State \Return $\mathcal{D}_{S \to T}$ (dependence polyhedron)
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

